## Literature Review (draft paragraph)

Whether shade trees protect smallholder coffee yields during drought is central to agroforestry recommendations in West Java. Evidence from smallholder plots indicates that shade is not uniformly beneficial but is strongly conditional on rainfall. In a normal year, shaded plots yielded 6 percent less cherry per bush than unshaded plots, suggesting a modest yield penalty from competition for light and nutrients where water is adequate. This penalty reverses under water stress: in the drought year, shaded plots lost 11 percent of their normal-year yield while unshaded plots lost 27 percent, so shade roughly halved the drought-induced yield loss. A plausible mechanism is microclimatic buffering and improved soil water retention; in the driest month of 2025, mean soil moisture was 24.1 percent in shaded plots and 17.6 percent in unshaded plots. These findings imply that the value of shade for smallholders is best framed as drought insurance rather than as a means of raising average yields.

